{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c5dfe020-6597-415e-a321-d7770deced8b","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A free-body diagram shows only forces acting on one object. Here, a block is pulled right across a rough, horizontal surface.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Newton's second law"},{"op":"diagram","id":"block","region":"right","primitives":[{"kind":"rect","from":[0.4,0.4],"to":[0.6,0.6],"stroke":"solid","label":"2 kg"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.15,0.62],[0.85,0.62]],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"horizontal supporting surface"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c5dfe020-6597-415e-a321-d7770deced8b","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Weight acts down and the normal force acts up. With no vertical acceleration, these forces balance.","ops":[{"op":"arrow","id":"normal","canvas_id":"block","from":[0.5,0.4],"to":[0.5,0.15],"stroke":"solid","label":"N"},{"op":"arrow","id":"weight","canvas_id":"block","from":[0.5,0.6],"to":[0.5,0.88],"stroke":"solid","label":"mg"},{"op":"equation","id":"vertical","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\sum F_y=N-mg=0"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c5dfe020-6597-415e-a321-d7770deced8b","step":{"id":"s3","script":"The pull is ten newtons right, while friction is four newtons left. Choose right as positive and subtract opposing forces.","ops":[{"op":"arrow","id":"pull","canvas_id":"block","from":[0.6,0.5],"to":[0.92,0.5],"stroke":"solid","label":"10 N"},{"op":"arrow","id":"friction","canvas_id":"block","from":[0.4,0.5],"to":[0.14,0.5],"stroke":"solid","label":"4 N"},{"op":"equation","id":"net","anchor":{"el":"vertical","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\sum F_x=10-4=6\\,\\mathrm{N}"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Which direction does the block accelerate, and why?","expected_gist":"Right, because the net force is six newtons to the right."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c5dfe020-6597-415e-a321-d7770deced8b","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Newton's second law connects net force to acceleration. Divide six newtons by two kilograms: the acceleration is three meters per second squared, rightward.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"law","anchor":{"el":"net","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\sum F_x=ma_x"},{"op":"equation","id":"answer","anchor":{"el":"law","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"a_x=\\frac{6\\,\\mathrm{N}}{2\\,\\mathrm{kg}}=3\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\quad\\text{rightward}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
